body:
The Houthaven was dug in 1876 as Amsterdam's first excavated harbour, for unloading and storing timber. From 2010 the city turned the old timber yards into a district of seven car-light islands, cut out of the land behind the Spaarndammerdijk and laid out along the lines of the old timber docks. The plan came from city planner Ton Schaap and architect Sjoerd Soeters. Soeters then wrote the rules: a 2007 image-quality plan (*beeldkwaliteitplan*) asked for "warm and informal" architecture in materials that age well, such as brick and timber, and a character of its own for every island. He stayed on as supervisor while a crowd of developers and architects filled the plots.

The characters have names. Wiborgeiland is *Stoere Jongens* ("tough guys", after the canal warehouses), Narva-eiland *Vrijbuiters* ("free spirits"), Memeleiland *Deftige dames* ("posh ladies"), Libau-eiland *Timmerlieden* ("carpenters") and Karlskrona-eiland *Rode Rondingen* ("red curves"). The result runs from sturdy warehouse blocks to fronts in the manner of Dudok and the Amsterdam School, mostly four or five storeys high. Islands, bridges and the eight new canals are named after Baltic ports of the timber trade.

The edges are less polite. Along the Haparandaweg, Blok 0 was split into nine self-build plots and built taller on purpose, as a sound wall against the port. Through traffic was buried in the Spaarndammertunnel (2018), with the Houthavenpark on its roof. The canals filled with IJ water from 2023; the last dam stuck fast and was cut open with an angle grinder in July 2026. About 2,700 homes, a fifth of them social rent, in a district meant to be climate neutral. The streets are public, the homes private. Part of the same ensemble: the Pontsteiger, Lariks, the Waterplein with the Zen Mast, and the Air cells in the park.

Look for:
- the Gevlebrug: Piet Kramer's 1930 lift bridge, which stood near Centraal Station, was stored for years and came back here in 2020; it no longer lifts, and the newer bridges around it copy its look;
- the shift in style as you cross from one island to the next;
- PUUUR Blok in Blok 0, a self-build block that won the Zuiderkerkprijs for the best Amsterdam housing of 2015;
- The Future on Wiborgeiland, 17 flats on the district's smallest plot that make more energy than they use.
